<%#
  The ⓘ that explains a control.

  Used instead of a paragraph of help under every setting: a page of those
  reads as a page of warnings, and the explanation is only wanted the first
  time or two.

  Three things about the markup, all deliberate:

  - **The focusable element wraps both parts**, so the explanation is shown on
    hover *and* on keyboard focus. A tooltip only a mouse can reach is one
    half the people cannot read.
  - **The bubble is hidden with `opacity`, never `display: none` or
    `visibility`.** Those two take it out of the accessibility tree as well as
    off the screen; opacity leaves it readable by a screen reader at all
    times, which is the simplest way to be sure the text is never lost.
  - **It regains pointer events when shown**, so a long explanation can be
    moved onto and read rather than vanishing on the way.

  Takes `text` (escaped) or `html` (raw, for an explanation with a link or a
  `<code>` in it). Nothing that prevents a mistake belongs in here - a warning
  or a limit stays on the page where it cannot be missed.
-%>
<span class="info" tabindex="0">
  <span class="info__mark" aria-hidden="true">i</span>
  <span class="info__bubble" role="note"><% if (locals.html) { %><%- html %><% } else { %><%= locals.text %><% } %></span>
</span>
